{"action": "final", "output": {
  "research_concepts": [
    "defensive suppression trigger",
    "suppression-conditional recommended mix",
    "adherence to recommended mix",
    "playoff series winning"
  ],
  "methodological_questions": [
    "How to measure adherence to the recommended mix without look-ahead leakage?",
    "How to control for team quality and opponent strength when predicting series win?",
    "Can we achieve adequate power with only 54 series?",
    "How reliable are play type and suppression labels for this analysis?",
    "Does the effect of adherence differ by play type (e.g., CUT vs non-CUT)?"
  ],
  "historical_evidence": [
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-APPM-001",
      "status_as_recorded": "supported",
      "finding": "Holdout games where a team's play-type mix is closer to the model's recommended mix score more efficiently. Strength-controlled gap +3.27 ORtg persists after team/opponent residualization."
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-APPS-023",
      "status_as_recorded": "rejected",
      "finding": "Matchup-specific non-CUT BEST counters do not beat a feasibility-weighted league mix that ignores game state (gap=+0.010, p=0.1709, n=136). Headline BEST advantage is CUT-driven."
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-APPS-017",
      "status_as_recorded": "not a hypothesis test",
      "finding": "Observed play-type mix often matches not the top-ranked recommended opener; average |delta| 4.5-24.0pp on leverage buckets; 4 of 8 sides best matched opener #3, not rank-1."
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF08",
      "status_as_recorded": "not a hypothesis test",
      "finding": "Teams' post-trigger BEST share varies widely (BOS 15.7% BEST, win 70% to ORL AVOID 59.3%, 0% win); win% not identified with BEST share (IND 4.5% BEST, 71% win)."
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-APPA-005",
      "status_as_recorded": "untestable",
      "finding": "Playoff defenses change scheme within and between games at high rates, but near-uniform transition matrices suggest the inferred scheme labels are noisy and cannot separate real adjustment from offensive execution changes."
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-APPS-058",
      "status_as_recorded": "untestable",
      "finding": "NBA matchup fields labeled only 14.9% and 3.3% of on-ball coverages at confident rates; switches_on unpopulated. Label error and small sample make scheme identification unusable at scale."
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-APPM-055",
      "status_as_recorded": "inconclusive",
      "finding": "The best counter after a suppressed play type is not stable from population to team to matchup levels; small samples and mechanical floor risk limit conclusions."
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-APPA-050",
      "status_as_recorded": "not a hypothesis test",
      "finding": "Certain play types (PUTBACK) suppress star next-possession PPP, but no multiplicity correction, small after-samples (n_after=32-95)."
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-APPS-012",
      "status_as_recorded": "not a hypothesis test",
      "finding": "Playoff defenses suppress DRIVE more than regular season (PPP -0.268, share -3.0pp); PNR share jumps +12.1pp, possibly from tagging differences."
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF09",
      "status_as_recorded": "inconclusive",
      "finding": "Double-suppression stacking (POST_UP then PUTBACK) shows less than additive effect, but implementation bugs and small samples limit reliability."
    }
  ],
  "failed_approaches": [
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-APPA-005",
      "what_failed": "Using inferred defensive scheme labels to study scheme changes",
      "why": "Label error (near-uniform transition matrices consistent with noise); unable to separate real adjustment from offensive execution variation"
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-APPS-058",
      "what_failed": "Labeling on-ball defensive coverage types from NBA matchup fields",
      "why": "Very low confident-label rates (14.9% and 3.3%); switches_on field unpopulated; label error and sample size made test untestable"
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-APPS-023",
      "what_failed": "Testing non-CUT matchup-specific counter effectiveness",
      "why": "Powered null result (gap=+0.010, p=0.1709, n=136); sample size and potential RTM selection issues"
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-APPS-017",
      "what_failed": "Assessing conformance to the top recommended opener",
      "why": "Mechanical floor: observed mix often matched lower-ranked openers; single-game horizon with no outcome comparison"
    }
  ],
  "evidence_establishes": [
    "Adherence to the recommended mix improves per-possession offensive efficiency (SF-APPM-001).",
    "The efficiency improvement from the recommended mix is largely driven by CUT plays; non-CUT matchup-specific counters show little benefit (SF-APPS-023).",
    "Teams do not consistently use the absolute best-ranked counter; conformance is noisy (SF-APPS-017).",
    "At the season level, BEST share does not clearly distinguish winning teams from losing teams (SF08).",
    "Defensive scheme labels from available matchup data are too unreliable for large-scale analysis (SF-APPA-005, SF-APPS-058)."
  ],
  "evidence_does_not_establish": [
    "Whether series-level adherence to the recommended mix predicts series win, controlling for team strength and other confounders.",
    "The magnitude of any adherence-win relationship, or its persistence after restricting to non-CUT plays.",
    "That the suppression-conditional recommended mix itself is stable enough across playoff rounds to be a valid target for adherence measurement (SF-APPM-055).",
    "That offensive efficiency gains from adherence translate to a detectable increase in series win probability in a sample of 54 series."
  ],
  "own_inferences": [
    "The small number of playoff series (54) likely limits power to detect a realistic effect, even if one exists.",
    "Any naive analysis without strict temporal holdout for the recommended mix would suffer from look-ahead leakage, producing optimistic but invalid results.",
    "The effect of adherence on winning is likely small and mediated by many other factors (opponent quality, injuries, game variance), making it hard to isolate.",
    "The recommended mix may need to be defined excluding CUT plays (or treating them separately) to avoid a mechanical floor artifact, as suggested by SF-APPS-023."
  ],
  "uncertainties": [
    "We do not know what proportion of playoff series contain enough suppression triggers to compute a reliable adherence metric.",
    "The reliability of play type classification (including inferrer step) in playoff high-pressure contexts has not been separately validated.",
    "Team strength and opponent strength could confound the adherence-win relationship if stronger teams both adhere more and win more; residualization may not fully remove this.",
    "The temporal stability of suppression triggers and recommended mixes across playoff rounds is unknown, which could add noise to the adherence measure."
  ],
  "historical_effect": {
    "changed_decision": "no_historical_evidence",
    "explanation": "No record directly tests the series-win prediction. The retrieved evidence informs design choices (e.g., temporal holdout to avoid leakage, need to control for CUT, use of efficiency as a mechanistic check) and highlights risks (label noise, small power), but does not change the underlying decision that a carefully designed test is theoretically possible. Without the historical evidence, one might have overlooked the CUT-driven nature of the advantage and the importance of strict pre-series computation of the recommended mix."
  },
  "testability": {
    "p_testable": 0.55,
    "blocking_constraints": [
      {
        "code": "SAMPLE_SIZE_POWER",
        "text": "Only 54 playoff series across four seasons (2022-23 to 2025-26) provide limited statistical power to detect a modest effect of adherence on series win probability."
      },
      {
        "code": "CONFOUNDING",
        "text": "Team strength and opponent strength are likely confounders; controlling for them in the small sample may leave residual confounding or drastically reduce degrees of freedom."
      },
      {
        "code": "LEAKAGE_LOOKAHEAD",
        "text": "The recommended mix must be computed strictly from data available before the series begins to avoid predicting the future. Any leakage would invalidate the test."
      },
      {
        "code": "MEASURE_RELIABILITY",
        "text": "Play type classification and suppression trigger definitions rely on rule-based inferrer steps that may misclassify, especially in noisy playoff contexts."
      },
      {
        "code": "EXPOSURE_SPARSITY",
        "text": "Some series may have few defensible suppression-trigger possessions, leading to high-variance adherence estimates and potential missing data."
      }
    ]
  },
  "prior_work": {
    "already_answered": "partly",
    "cited": [
      "FAM:SF-APPM-001",
      "FAM:SF-APPS-023",
      "FAM:SF-APPS-017",
      "FAM:SF08"
    ],
    "summary": "Prior work establishes that adherence to the recommended mix improves offensive efficiency (SF-APPM-001), but that effect is largely driven by CUT plays (SF-APPS-023). No prior study directly tests whether series-level adherence predicts series win. Descriptive patterns (SF08, SF-APPS-017) suggest a weak or noisy relationship between raw BEST-share and winning, but those analyses did not control for triggers, opponent quality, or temporal leakage. The current question is partly informed by these findings but remains untested."
  },
  "risks": [
    {
      "code": "SAMPLE_SIZE_POWER",
      "text": "With 54 series, effect sizes would need to be large to be reliably detected; a realistic ORtg-to-win translation may be too small."
    },
    {
      "code": "CONFOUNDING",
      "text": "Better teams may naturally adhere more and win more, even if adherence has no causal effect. Pre-series strength metrics will be imperfect proxies."
    },
    {
      "code": "LEAKAGE_LOOKAHEAD",
      "text": "Must ensure the recommended mix for a series is computed using only data from prior to that series; using any in-series data to define the mix would create circularity."
    },
    {
      "code": "MEASURE_RELIABILITY",
      "text": "The suppression triggers and play type labels may have noise that attenuates any true adherence-win relationship."
    },
    {
      "code": "MULTIPLE_TESTING",
      "text": "There are many ways to define adherence (share of BEST, distance to recommended mix, rank weighting); freedom to choose post-hoc could inflate Type I error."
    },
    {
      "code": "RTM_SELECTION",
      "text": "Suppression triggers are defined by extreme H1-to-H2 PPP drops; some may reflect regression to the mean rather than genuine defensive adjustments, adding noise."
    }
  ],
  "predicted_outcome": {
    "supported": 0.25,
    "rejected": 0.2,
    "untestable": 0.15,
    "artifact": 0.15,
    "uncertain": 0.25
  },
  "decision": "proceed_with_changes",
  "design": {
    "estimand": "The effect of a team's average per-game adherence to its pre-series suppression-conditional recommended mix on its probability of winning a playoff series, controlling for pre-series team strength and opponent strength.",
    "unit": "Playoff series (1 observation per series, using the difference in adherence between the two teams; N≈54).",
    "sample": "All completed playoff series from the 2022-23 through 2025-26 NBA postseasons. Exclude series where fewer than 20% of games contain at least one suppression trigger for either team, or where pre-series recommended mix cannot be reliably computed (e.g., too few regular-season triggers).",
    "power": "Under optimistic assumptions (adherence difference between winners and losers of 0.1 standard deviations, corresponding to a 5-percentage-point increase in win probability), power is approximately 0.5-0.6 with a one-sided test at α=0.05 using logistic regression. We will report an exact power analysis via simulation based on observed trigger frequencies.",
    "controls": "Pre-series team strength (regular-season net rating, weighted recency), opponent pre-series strength, home-court advantage (games at higher seed), and a residualized adherence metric that regresses out team identity and season to limit persistent style confounds.",
    "falsification": "1. Placebo test: replace the recommended mix with a random mix generated from the same distribution of play types. 2. CUT exclusion: test whether the result holds when CUT possessions are removed from the adherence calculation, given SF-APPS-023. 3. Temporal check: if the effect is genuine, adherence should not predict series wins when the recommended mix is computed using future data (look-ahead leakage check).",
    "multiplicity": "Pre-register one primary adherence metric (post-trigger share of possessions using the BEST counter) and one primary model specification. Any alternative metrics or sensitivity tests will be clearly labeled as exploratory with appropriate correction.",
    "decision_rule": "Reject the null hypothesis (no positive effect of adherence) if the logistic regression coefficient for the adherence difference is positive with a one-sided p-value < 0.05, and the result survives the pre-registered robustness check (CUT exclusion or placebo). The series-level effect must be consistent in direction with the within-game efficiency effect observed in SF-APPM-001.",
    "data_version": "NBA stats API data frozen as of 2026-06-16. Recommended mixes for each team and series are computed using only regular-season and earlier-round data from the same postseason, plus the previous two regular seasons, without any data from the target series or later. All trigger and mix computations are versioned and reproducible from the code repository."
  }
}}

